Exam prep 1 

Likely questions by topic (simple English, as you prefer):
1. Hands-on wet lab experience
Tell me about a wet lab technique you have done yourself (PCR, qPCR, gel, cell culture, extraction).
Describe an experiment that failed. What went wrong and how did you fix it?
What controls do you use in PCR or qPCR, and why?
How do you avoid contamination?
If you have no bench experience in some area, say so honestly and explain what you understand theoretically.
2. Research design and scientific judgment
How would you design an experiment to test whether gene X causes disease Y?
How many replicates do you need, and why?
What is the difference between a biological and a technical replicate?
How do you avoid batch effect and confounding at the design stage?
A result looks too good. What do you check first?
3. Analysis of biological data
Walk me through a bulk RNA-seq analysis from raw reads to DE genes.
Why FDR and not raw p-values?
Why raw counts and not TPM for DE?
How do you check variant quality (Ti/Tv)?
What is pseudoreplication in single-cell data?
How do you spot data leakage in an ML model?
4. Connecting bench and computation
How would you validate a computational finding at the bench (for example, a DE gene by qPCR)?
A wet lab result disagrees with your pipeline. What do you do?
How does sample prep quality (RNA integrity, library prep) affect your analysis?
How would you explain a bioinformatics result to a bench scientist?
5. Custom job questions (AI evaluation)
How do you judge if an AI answer in biology is right or wrong?
What makes a good benchmark question?
Give an example of an AI hallucination you caught.
How do you make your work reproducible?
What is the weakness of your projects?
Answer structure that works: answer in one line first, then one reason, then one real example from your projects. Keep each answer to about 60 to 90 seconds, since time per question is limited.
If you want, I can run a mock interview: I ask one question at a time and you answer, then I give feedback.







